Recursive synthetic data training causes model collapse, manifested spectrally as monotonic decline in the effective rank and spectral log-volume of learned representations.
Existing approaches either diagnose collapse post-hoc or apply static real-data mixing ratios that ignore its non-linear acceleration across generations.
We provide, to our knowledge, the first spectral-level characterization of how effective rank evolves across recursive generations, and propose a closed-loop adaptive controller that redistributes a fixed real-data budget temporally based on spectral health feedback.
Concretely, we define spectral log-volume (SLV), the log-determinant of the representation covariance, as a continuous collapse indicator and derive an online early warning signal (EWS) from its normalized decline rate.
Our controller applies reactive boosts when the SLV decline rate exceeds a calibrated threshold, while total real-data consumption equals that of a uniform baseline.
We evaluate spectral-guided adaptive allocation on Pythia-410M across 10 recursive generations. \textbf{[TBD: key finding]}
Our framework provides practitioners with an automated, budget-neutral mechanism to balance synthetic data efficiency against representation quality degradation in recursive training pipelines.
\end{abstract}
